-- title:
--   Frobenius conjugacy between the actions of (g,α) and (g,α^q)
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, let $k$ be a perfect field equipped with an algebra structure over $\mathrm{GaloisField}\ q\ 2$ (the field of $q^2$ elements), and assume the coordinate ring [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q k`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21), the quotient of the polynomial ring in two variables over $k$ by the principal ideal generated by `drinfeldPoly q k - 1`, is a domain; let $\ell$ be a prime and let $E$ be a field with a $\mathbb{Q}_\ell$-algebra structure. Write $F$ for `drinfeldFunctionField q k`, the fraction field of that coordinate ring, and $V = \mathbb{Q}_\ell \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell} T_\ell$ for the rational Tate module of the group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of degree-zero divisors on the places of $F$ over $k$ modulo principal divisors, where $T_\ell$ consists of the sequences $(x_n)$ with $\ell^n x_n = 0$ and $\ell x_{n+1} = x_n$. The representation `tateRep q k ℓ E` sends an element of the subgroup $H$ of pairs $(g,\alpha) \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q) \times \mathbb{F}_{q^2}^\times$ with $\det(g)\,\alpha^{q+1} = 1$ to its $E$-linear endomorphism of $E \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}_\ell} V$, obtained from the action of $H$ on $F$ by $k$-algebra automorphisms, the induced action on the rational Tate module, and base change to $E$. The assertion is that there is an $E$-linear automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$ of $E \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}_\ell} V$ such that, for every $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q)$ and $\alpha \in \mathbb{F}_{q^2}^\times$ with both $(g,\alpha) \in H$ and $(g,\alpha^q) \in H$, the underlying linear map of $\mathrm{Fr}$ satisfies $\mathrm{Fr} \circ \rho(g,\alpha) = \rho(g,\alpha^q) \circ \mathrm{Fr}$, where $\rho =$ `tateRep q k ℓ E`.
--
--   This expresses that the $H$-representation on the rational $\ell$-adic Tate module of the Jacobian of the Drinfeld curve is isomorphic to its twist by the Frobenius substitution $(g,\alpha) \mapsto (g,\alpha^q)$ on the torus parameter; the membership of $(g,\alpha^q)$ in $H$ is taken as a hypothesis rather than deduced. It is used in the construction of linear maps on rational Tate modules attached to semistable coverings of modular curves of full level, in the three statements [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_linearMap_rationalTateModule_drinfeld_injective_comp_eq_of_inducesOnChart_of_semistableCovering`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_linearMap_rationalTateModule_drinfeld_injective_comp_eq_of_inducesOnChart_of_semistableCovering) and its variants for the cases $q = 2$ and $q = 3$.
